+# ADR 0026 — The team server: `wizardingcode-mem serve`
+
+Date: 2026-11-10 · Status: **draft**. This is part 5 of the knowledge and team journey (2026-11-08-001): the server a team runs on its own host, built and tested in-process, with its image and compose file. The sync client (part 6) and the runbook (part 7) are not built: no client calls this server yet, and nothing on a person's machine sends anything. The twenty-six rulings below were taken with the author on 2026-11-10 (SQLite; several servers per person, a project bound to one; the fastest sync; no token expiry; purge as the operator's; the first slice; the mirror as an export) or recorded as the specification's stated choices; where this record and the specification differ, this record is the later one.
+
+## Context
+
+Parts 1 to 4 built the knowledge store ([ADR 0024](0024-knowledge-module.md)) and the sharing policy ([ADR 0025](0025-sharing-policy.md)): what may leave a person's machine is decided, and nothing leaves. The author asked for the other end: one Docker image on the team's VPS where members' agents share knowledge entries and the memories a project's level allows, in near real time, with the repositories on the volume readable by a person. The author also asked for several servers per person (one for the WizardingCode projects, another for a client's), bound per project, and for the sync to be as fast as it can so the team's work becomes collaborative.
+
+## Decision
+
+`wizardingcode-mem serve` is a long-running process over its own SQLite database, `team.db`, in a data directory of its own, one process per directory. Members join with single-use invitation codes and hold one device token per machine. A device pushes items one by one (`POST /v1/push`) and pulls the feed by a cursor (`GET /v1/pull`), holding the pull open for up to 25 seconds so a change made on one machine reaches another within a second. Every item is kept with every revision; a withdraw is a tombstone; erasure is the operator's `serve purge`. The repositories under `repos/` are an export of the entries, written from the database by `serve mirror`, which the server runs as a child process and the operator runs by hand. The image runs as user 10001 behind Caddy, which terminates TLS. The whole of the server's SQL is one module.
+
+## Rulings
+
+| Ruling | Decision | Why | Cost if wrong |
+| :- | :- | :- | :- |
+| D-1 | The server's data is its own: `team.db` with its own migration list and `user_version`, and the repositories under its data directory; never a client's store. Every SQL statement lives in `src/team/store.ts`, behind one `transaction`, but for what an open needs before the store can be asked, in `db.ts`: the version read, the schema check, `secure_delete` and migration 1's identity write. | The author chose SQLite, behind a boundary that admits another engine later; one file holds what such a change would touch. | The change signal, the leases, the rate limiter and the high-water file also assume one process ([One process](#one-process-one-store-boundary)): a second engine is not a drop-in. |
+| D-2 | A device token is 32 random bytes shown once, stored as its SHA-256 digest, which is the lookup key; revocation sets a time and deletes nothing. | A digest cannot be read back; the device's history stays attributable. | No salt: a leaked database lets a token be checked, not recovered. |
+| D-3 | The first admin's invitation is minted at the first start with no members and printed once as a JSON log line, valid 24 hours; expired or lost, `serve invite --role admin --name <name>` mints another. | The log is the one channel a fresh container has. | The code is in the container's log until rotated ([The boundary](#the-boundary)). |
+| D-4 | Invitations are single-use 130-bit codes with a role, the member's name and a 72-hour expiry (1 to 720), made by an admin through the API or the CLI, stored hashed; no open sign-up, no e-mail. | A team is invited, not discovered. | Every member costs an admin one call. |
+| D-5 | Members push, pull, rotate and read; admins also invite, list, revoke, disable, enable, set roles, withdraw and reinstate any item; the last active admin cannot be revoked, disabled or demoted, and the refusal says how to go on. Purging is the operator's. | A team must never lock itself out. | An admin who wants to leave promotes someone first. |
+| D-6 | JSON over HTTP under `/v1/`; push answers per item; pull by a monotonic cursor with `next` and `more`; a request id on every answer. | A client reconciles item by item and resumes from a number. | None found. |
+| D-7 | An entry push carries the revision and hash it was built on; both must equal the current ones, else `stale` with the current ones; a first send carries revision 0; a withdraw carries them too. | A device never overwrites text it has not read. | A client behind by one revision pulls before it pushes again. |
+| D-8 | Stored: part 4's payload types plus member, device, invitation and audit rows, the revision, the hash and the times; every revision kept; the entry keyed by its UUID; the payload checked against part 4's pinned key lists and by type, its hash recomputed. | The server keeps nothing it was not sent and knows the shape of what it keeps. | Storage grows with every revision ([Follow-ups](#follow-ups)). |
+| D-9 | An invitation may be bound to a member, so a join with it adds a device; device ids are random and independent of the token. | One person, several machines, one identity. | Only an admin adds a device. |
+| D-10 | The database is the authority; the sync never reads the mirror. | One truth, rebuildable exports. | A repository on the volume is for people, not for the sync. |
+| D-11 | A device pulls the shared and every member's workspace entries always, and project items only for the remote aliases it names; one current row per key since the cursor; 500 items or 2 MiB a page; a snapshot of one alias with `scope=project`; a tombstone in the range an item leaves when it moves. | A machine holds what its projects need and no more. | The alias filter keeps a machine small; it is not a permission ([The API](#the-api)). |
+| D-12 | Shared and project entries are the team's, written by any member on the current base; a workspace entry is written, moved or withdrawn by its creator's devices alone, and only the creator moves an entry into `workspace`; a memory is written by the device that first sent it and withdrawn by that device, another device of its member, or an admin; an entry is withdrawn by its creator's member or an admin, on the current base; an admin's withdraw locks until reinstated; a re-send on the tombstone's base reinstates. | The stored row decides ownership, never the uid alone, so a rename of a remote or a move never splits a history. | The creator of a workspace entry is the one person who can take it back. |
+| D-13 | `AUTOINCREMENT` sequences; every answer carries `serverId` and `instance`; the instance is renewed when a restored database is detected and by `serve instance renew`; every renewal advances the feed's sequence by 1,000,000; a cursor ahead of the sequence is 410, pull from zero. | A client must never read a reused number as the row it had. | One undetected case ([Restores](#the-server-id-the-instance-and-restores)). |
+| D-14 | Prefixes `wcm1_` and `wci1_` that `redact` recognises; a device rotates its own token in one call; revoking a device cancels its unused invitations; join is rate-limited per address, the forwarded address trusted only with `--trust-proxy`. | A pasted token or code is caught by the client's own redaction. | Single-phase rotation: a lost answer means a re-join. |
+| D-15 | 1 MiB body, 200 items a push, 256 KiB an item, 500 items or 2 MiB a page, 600 requests a minute a device, 10 joins a minute an address, 64 MiB of applied payload an hour a device, a wait of 25 s at most, `refused storage` under 200 MiB free, health 503 under 100 MiB; published in `/v1/me`; the buckets in memory, reset on restart. | A looping device cannot fill the disk in minutes. | No stored quota ([Follow-ups](#follow-ups)). |
+| D-16 | One image on a digest-pinned `debian:bookworm-slim` with git and ca-certificates, the Linux binary per architecture copied executable, user 10001, `/data` as the volume, the binary's own health probe; loopback by default, `0.0.0.0` set by the image; the compose file runs Docker's init, sets trust-proxy and runs Caddy with HSTS; the release pushes the image to the GitHub container registry under a lower-case name. | The compose file, not the image, decides what runs in front. | No init and no proxy trust when the image runs alone. |
+| D-17 | `serve` joins the lazy command table, skips the legacy data migration, handles SIGTERM and SIGINT, logs JSON lines to standard output without tokens, codes or payloads; idle timeout 120 s; one running server per data directory by a lease (`serve.lease`, a `serve-<16 hex>` owner, 30 s, renewed every 10 s). | A container's pids repeat; a lease with an owner does not. | After a crash the next start waits out the lease ([The lease](#the-leases-30-second-window)). |
+| D-18 | The client names its contract in `X-WCM-Contract`; another value is 409 with the accepted list; the client speaks the highest the server accepts; from the first bump the server also accepts the one before for a release; the key lists, result shapes and reason codes are pinned by a fingerprint test. | Either side may upgrade first. | A change to a shape without a bump fails the build, by design. |
